  title="Clean, Repair, or Replace? Deciding What to Do With Rental Carpet at Turnover"
  description="How to decide whether rental carpet needs a cleaning, a repair, or replacement at turnover, plus pet odor, switching to hard flooring, and deposit deductions."
>
<p>At a rental turnover, clean the carpet when it is structurally sound and the stains are on the surface, repair it when the damage is limited to a few spots or ripples, and replace it when it is near the end of its useful life, when pet urine has soaked into the pad, or when the damage covers too much of the room to patch cleanly. The test that settles most cases is simple: after a professional cleaning, would the next tenant walk in and feel the floor is clean and in good shape? If yes, clean it. If the carpet will still look tired, smell, or need replacing within a year or two, replacing it now, while the unit is empty and the other trades are already scheduled, usually saves a second disruption later.</p>

<h2>Start with an honest look at the carpet</h2>
<p>Walk every carpeted room at move out, in good light, and check these five things:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Age.</strong> When was it installed? Your property file or the last flooring invoice should say. Carpet in a rental takes more wear than carpet in an owner occupied home, and its useful life is shorter.</li>
<li><strong>Wear patterns.</strong> Look at the hallways, the path from the door to the couch, and the bottom of the stairs. Matted, crushed fibers that do not spring back are worn out, and cleaning will not fix them.</li>
<li><strong>Stains.</strong> Surface stains from spills often come out with hot water extraction. Bleach spots, dye stains and burns usually do not.</li>
<li><strong>Odor.</strong> Get down close to the floor in corners and along walls, especially if there were pets. A smell that lingers in an empty room often means something has reached the pad.</li>
<li><strong>Structure.</strong> Look for ripples, loose seams, frayed edges at doorways and loose carpet on stairs.</li>
</ol>
<p>Take photos of everything you find. They help you make the call, and they support any security deposit decision.</p>

<h2>When cleaning is the right call</h2>
<p>Professional cleaning, usually hot water extraction, makes sense when:</p>
<ul>
<li>The carpet still has good life left</li>
<li>The fibers spring back when you press on them</li>
<li>Stains are from ordinary spills and traffic dirt</li>
<li>There is no lingering odor after the unit has aired out</li>
<li>Seams and edges are intact</li>
</ul>
<p>Schedule the carpet cleaning near the end of the turnover, after painting and repairs are done, so paint drips and drywall dust do not undo it. Our <a href="/articles/rental-make-ready-checklist/">rental make ready checklist</a> shows where it fits.</p>
<p>Spot treat heavy stains before the full cleaning. Stains that have been cleaned once and wicked back up as the carpet dried may need a second treatment. Tell the cleaner about any known pet areas so they can bring the right treatment.</p>

<h2>When repair is the right call</h2>
<p>Some damage is limited enough to fix without replacing a whole room.</p>
<h3>Ripples and loose carpet</h3>
<p>Carpet that has bunched up or developed waves can usually be stretched back into place with a power stretcher. Ripples are a trip hazard, so do not leave them for the next tenant. Stretching is also often a good time to check that the tack strip along the walls is still holding.</p>
<h3>Small burns, tears or stains in one spot</h3>
<p>A small damaged area can sometimes be patched by cutting out the damaged section and replacing it with a matching piece. The best source for a match is often a remnant left from the original install or a piece from the back of a closet, where it will not be seen. A patch works best on carpet that is still in good condition overall. On worn carpet, a new patch can stand out because it looks newer than the floor around it.</p>
<h3>Loose seams and frayed transitions</h3>
<p>Seams that have opened and edges that fray at doorways or where carpet meets hard flooring can usually be resealed or fitted with a new transition strip.</p>

<h2>When replacement is the right call</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pet urine in the pad.</strong> When urine soaks through the carpet into the pad, and sometimes into the subfloor, surface cleaning rarely removes the odor for good. The usual fix is to remove the carpet and pad in the affected area, treat or seal the subfloor, and install new pad and carpet.</li>
<li><strong>Worn out traffic areas.</strong> When the main walking paths are matted and threadbare, the carpet has reached the end of its life.</li>
<li><strong>Too much damage to patch.</strong> Multiple stains, burns or tears across a room usually look worse patched than replaced.</li>
<li><strong>Age.</strong> Carpet near the end of its expected life will need replacing soon anyway. Doing it during the current vacancy avoids pulling furniture and disrupting a tenant mid lease. Our article on <a href="/articles/how-long-rental-finishes-last/">how long rental finishes last</a> covers typical lifespans for carpet and other finishes.</li>
<li><strong>Water damage.</strong> Carpet and pad that have been soaked by a leak can grow mold if they do not dry quickly. Find and fix the leak first, then evaluate the flooring.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Replace with carpet or switch to hard flooring?</h2>
<p>If you are replacing anyway, it is worth asking whether carpet is still the right floor for that room. Many rental owners in Middle Tennessee are moving to luxury vinyl plank in living areas, hallways and kitchens because it is water resistant, easy to clean between tenants, and usually does not need replacing at every turn. Carpet still has a place in bedrooms, where tenants often prefer something softer and warmer underfoot.</p>
<p>A few things to think about:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Who lives there.</strong> Pets and young kids are hard on carpet.</li>
<li><strong>What the market expects.</strong> Look at comparable rentals nearby. Hard floors in living areas have become common in many price ranges.</li>
<li><strong>Your hold period.</strong> A floor that lasts through several tenants is worth more to a long term owner.</li>
<li><strong>Consistency.</strong> Using the same product across your units makes repairs and future matches easier.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/services/flooring/">flooring services</a> page covers both carpet and hard flooring installs.</p>

<h2>Carpet and the security deposit</h2>
<p>In Tennessee, normal wear from everyday living is generally the landlord's cost, while damage beyond normal wear can be charged against the deposit. Traffic wear on a carpet that is several years old is normal wear. A large pet stain, burns or a torn section usually is not.</p>
<p>When carpet damage is charged to a tenant, many landlords prorate the charge based on how much useful life the carpet had left, so the tenant pays for the damage rather than for a brand new floor. Keep the move in photos, the move out photos, the age of the carpet and the invoice for the work. Our guide to <a href="/articles/wear-and-tear-vs-damage-tennessee/">wear and tear versus damage in Tennessee</a> explains how that line is usually drawn. For questions about a specific deposit dispute, a Tennessee landlord attorney is the right resource.</p>

<h2>A quick decision guide</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Good life left, surface dirt, no odor:</strong> clean</li>
<li><strong>Good life left, a ripple or one damaged spot:</strong> repair, then clean</li>
<li><strong>Odor that stays after the unit airs out:</strong> pull back a corner and check the pad, and plan to replace the affected area</li>
<li><strong>Worn traffic paths, near the end of its life, or widespread damage:</strong> replace, and consider whether hard flooring fits the room better</li>
</ul>
<p>Making the call during the move out walk through, instead of after the cleaning has been tried and failed, keeps the turnover moving and saves a wasted visit.</p>
